Specification CoreTypes

Description The types every other specification in SimpleAccount is written in.
Details Declared once here because the analyzer treats a spec folder as one
Details namespace; the other files refer to these names without redeclaring them.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Money
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

DataType Money
Description A signed amount in the currency of the book.
Details Two decimal places, held as whole cents so that sums are exact.
Details A leading dollar sign and thousands separators may be written and are
Details accepted on input; the stored value is the number.
Uses Decimal
Examples: ValidValues
| Value     | IsValid | Notes                              |
| 0.00      | true    | A zero amount is legal             |
| 12.34     | true    |                                    |
| -12.34    | true    | Negative is legal: a credit        |
| $1,234.56 | true    | Currency and separators accepted   |
| ($45.00)  | true    | Accounting parentheses mean -45.00 |
| 1.005     | false   | More than two decimal places       |
| 12.3.4    | false   | Not a number                       |
|           | false   | Empty is not zero                  |
| USD 12.34 | false   | No currency codes in this release  |

BusinessRule Money is held as whole cents
Description Parsing text into a stored integer number of cents.
Details Rounding is not applied: a third decimal place is rejected rather than
Details rounded, so that no cent is ever invented by an import.
Examples: MoneyParse
| Text      | Cents  | Notes                              |
| 0.00      | 0      |                                    |
| 12.34     | 1234   |                                    |
| -12.34    | -1234  |                                    |
| $1,234.56 | 123456 |                                    |
| ($45.00)  | -4500  | Parentheses are the negative sign  |
| .50       | 50     | Leading zero may be omitted        |
| 12        | 1200   | No decimal point means whole units |
| 12.3      | 1230   | One decimal place is tenths        |

Attributes MoneyParse
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Text      | String  |         |
| Cents     | Integer | 0       |
| Notes     | String  |         |

BusinessRule Money formats for display
Description How a stored amount is written back out.
Details Negative amounts show a leading minus in the register and parentheses
Details in reports, which is the convention each place is read in.
Examples: MoneyFormat
| Cents  | InRegister | InReport | Notes                    |
| 0      | 0.00     | 0.00     |                            |
| 1234   | 12.34    | 12.34    |                            |
| -1234  | -12.34   | (12.34)  | Two conventions, one value |
| 123456 | 1,234.56 | 1,234.56 | Grouped above a thousand   |

Attributes MoneyFormat
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Cents     | Integer | 0       |
| InRegister | String  |         |
| InReport   | String  |         |
| Notes     | String  |         |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Names and identifiers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

DataType AccountName
Description The name of one account, being one segment of its path.
Details A colon separates the segments of a path, so a name may not contain one.
Details Blanks around a name are trimmed before it is checked, so they can never
Details make a name invalid; " Checking" and "Checking" are the same name.
Uses Text
Examples: ValidValues
| Value         | IsValid | Notes                          |
| Checking      | true    |                                |
| Wells Fargo   | true    | Spaces inside a name are fine  |
| Auto & Travel | true    |                                |
| ~Checking     | true    | Trimmed to Checking            |
|               | false   | A name is required             |
| ~~~           | false   | Blanks alone are no name       |
| Expense:Fuel  | false   | A colon separates segments     |

DataType AccountPath
Description The full colon-separated path of an account, root segment first.
Details This is how every other specification refers to an account.
Uses Text
Examples: ValidValues
| Value              | IsValid | Notes                          |
| Assets:Checking    | true    |                                |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel | true    | Any depth is allowed           |
| Expenses           | true    | A root account is a valid path |
|                    | false   | A path is required             |
| Expenses:          | false   | Empty trailing segment         |
| :Expenses          | false   | Empty leading segment          |
| Expenses::Fuel     | false   | Empty middle segment           |

DataType PayeeName
Description Who the money went to, or came from.
Details May be empty: a bank fee or an opening balance has no payee.
Uses Text
Examples: ValidValues
| Value         | IsValid | Notes               |
| Harris Teeter | true    |                     |
|               | true    | A payee is optional |
| AT&T          | true    |                     |

DataType Symbol
Description The ticker symbol of a security, where one is known.
Details May be empty. A QIF export carries a security name and often no symbol
Details at all: of the 44 securities in the business file in testdata, not one
Details has a symbol. A holding is identified by its SecurityName, and the
Details symbol is an optional shorthand. See Investments.
Uses Text
Examples: ValidValues
| Value  | IsValid | Notes                               |
| VTSAX  | true    |                                     |
| BRK.B  | true    | A dot is part of some symbols       |
| vtsax  | true    | Folded to upper case on input       |
|        | true    | Unknown; the security has a name    |
| VT SAX | false   | No spaces in a symbol               |

DataType SecurityName
Description The name of a security, which is what a QIF export always gives.
Details Up to 76 characters in the business file in testdata, and all but one
Details of the 44 names contain spaces.
Uses Text
Examples: ValidValues
| Value                          | IsValid | Notes                          |
| ISHARES U S TRANSPORTATION ETF | true    | Spaces are ordinary here       |
| Vanguard Total Stock Mkt Idx   | true    |                                |
| VTSAX                          | true    | A name may look like a symbol  |
|                                | false   | A holding must name a security |

DataType Shares
Description A number of shares or units held.
Details Six decimal places, which is what fund statements report.
Details Never negative: this release does not model short positions.
Uses Decimal
Examples: ValidValues
| Value      | IsValid | Notes                         |
| 0          | true    | A closed position             |
| 100        | true    |                               |
| 12.345678  | true    | Six decimal places            |
| 12.3456789 | false   | Seven is more than is tracked |
| -5         | false   | No short positions            |

DataType Price
Description The price of one share.
Details Four decimal places, never negative.
Uses Decimal
Examples: ValidValues
| Value     | IsValid | Notes                   |
| 0         | true    | Unpriced                |
| 123.4567  | true    | Four decimal places     |
| 123.45678 | false   | Five is too many        |
| -1        | false   | A price is not negative |

DataType CheckNumber
Description The number written on a paper check.
Uses Text
Examples: ValidValues
| Value | IsValid | Notes                       |
| 1234  | true    |                             |
|       | true    | Most transactions have none |
| 1234A | true    | Some banks suffix a letter  |

DataType FitId
Description The identifier a bank assigns a transaction in an OFX file.
Details This is what lets a second import recognise what it has already seen.
Uses Text
Examples: ValidValues
| Value                | IsValid | Notes                        |
| 20240115001          | true    |                              |
| 2024-01-15-AMZN-4499 | true    | Banks choose their own shape |
|                      | false   | Required when OFX supplies it |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Enumerations
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

DataType AccountType
Description What kind of account a node in the tree is: the type a person
Description chooses, within the group it is chosen from.
Details The group is not stored anywhere. It follows from the type, so an
Details account filed once cannot drift out of the heading it is listed under;
Details see the group rule in UserInterface.
Details
Details Income and Expense are what Quicken calls categories, and modelling
Details them as accounts is what makes every transaction balance. Equity holds
Details the opening balances that start the book. None of the three is in a
Details group, because the account list does not show them.
Details
Details The names Bank, Asset, Liability and Investment were the whole of this
Details list before it was split into groups. They are still read, so that a
Details book written by an earlier build still opens, and are never written
Details again: Bank reads as Checking, Asset as OtherAsset, Liability as
Details OtherDebt and Investment as Brokerage.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value              | Notes                                                   |
| Checking           | A current account, written on and paid into             |
| Savings            | Interest and no cheques                                 |
| Cash               | A wallet, or petty cash                                 |
| CreditCard         | A balance owed, which rises as it is spent              |
| LineOfCredit       | Borrowed against as it is needed                        |
| Brokerage          | Holds securities as well as cash                        |
| IRA                | A retirement account                                    |
| HSA                | A health savings account                                |
| Retirement401k     | Written without the leading digit a name may not have   |
| Mortgage           | Secured on a property                                   |
| AutoLoan           | Secured on a vehicle                                    |
| PersonalLoan       | Unsecured                                               |
| OtherDebt          | Any other money owed                                    |
| RealEstate         | A house, a flat, land                                   |
| Vehicle            | A car                                                   |
| OtherAsset         | Anything else owned                                     |
| AccountsReceivable | Owed to the business                                    |
| AccountsPayable    | Owed by the business                                    |
| TransferIn         | Money on its way in, and off the balance sheet          |
| TransferOut        | Money on its way out, and off the balance sheet         |
| Income             | A category money arrives from                           |
| Expense            | A category money goes to                                |
| Equity             | Opening balances, so a new book starts in balance       |

DataType AccountClass
Description The two groups the account types fall into.
Details Reports ask this rather than listing the types.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value   | Notes                                                  |
| Real    | Everything that holds money, in any of the seven groups |
| Nominal | Income, Expense and Equity: the categories             |

BusinessRule Account class follows from account type
Description Which types are real and which are nominal. Every type that is in
Description a group is real, and the three that are in none are the categories.
Examples: AccountClassOf
| AccountType        | AccountClass | Notes                             |
| Checking           | Real         |                                   |
| Savings            | Real         |                                   |
| Cash               | Real         |                                   |
| CreditCard         | Real         |                                   |
| LineOfCredit       | Real         |                                   |
| Brokerage          | Real         |                                   |
| IRA                | Real         |                                   |
| HSA                | Real         |                                   |
| Retirement401k     | Real         |                                   |
| Mortgage           | Real         |                                   |
| AutoLoan           | Real         |                                   |
| PersonalLoan       | Real         |                                   |
| OtherDebt          | Real         |                                   |
| RealEstate         | Real         |                                   |
| Vehicle            | Real         |                                   |
| OtherAsset         | Real         |                                   |
| AccountsReceivable | Real         |                                   |
| AccountsPayable    | Real         |                                   |
| TransferIn         | Real         | Real, and still off the balance sheet |
| TransferOut        | Real         |                                   |
| Income             | Nominal      |                                   |
| Expense            | Nominal      |                                   |
| Equity             | Nominal      | Reconciling it would mean nothing |

Attributes AccountClassOf
| Attribute    | Type         | Default  |
| AccountType  | AccountType  | Checking |
| AccountClass | AccountClass | Real     |
| Notes        | String       |          |

BusinessRule The type an account starts as when its group is chosen
Description Making an account is choosing one of the seven groups, not one of
Description twenty-three types. Banking is the question a person can answer
Description while opening an account; whether it is a checking or a savings
Description account is a detail of the same answer, and it is settled afterwards
Description in the account's own dialog.
Details So each group has the type an account takes when only the group has
Details been said. Six of the seven have an obvious one -- the plainest member,
Details or the only one anybody opens by hand.
Details
Details Business is the exception and is worth knowing about: a receivable and
Details a payable are opposite sides of the sheet and neither is the plain one,
Details so an account made under Business starts as a receivable and a payable
Details has to be changed afterwards.
Examples: DefaultTypeOf
| AccountGroup     | AccountType        | Notes                              |
| Banking          | Checking           | Savings is chosen afterwards       |
| Credit           | CreditCard         |                                    |
| Investments      | Brokerage          | IRA, HSA and 401k afterwards       |
| LoanAndDebt      | OtherDebt          | A mortgage or a car loan afterwards |
| PropertyAndAsset | OtherAsset         | Real estate or a vehicle afterwards |
| Business         | AccountsReceivable | A payable has to be changed to     |
| Transfer         | TransferIn         |                                    |

Attributes DefaultTypeOf
| Attribute    | Type         | Default            |
| AccountGroup | AccountGroup | Banking            |
| AccountType  | AccountType  | Checking           |
| Notes        | String       | none               |

DataType BalanceSide
Description Which column of a balance sheet an account belongs in.
Details Asked only by the balance sheet. The account list says nothing about it
Details -- the words Asset and Liability are not headings there, the seven
Details groups are.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value     | Notes            |
| Asset     | What is owned    |
| Liability | What is owed     |

BusinessRule Which side of a balance sheet a type is on, and whether it is shown
Description Every real type has a side, because the side is also what says
Description which way round the account reads on screen. Being shown on the
Description balance sheet is a separate question, and the Transfer group
Description answers it differently from every other group.
Details A transfer account holds money on its way between two accounts that
Details are both already on the sheet, so counting it would count the same
Details money twice. It still has a side -- in reads as money held, out as
Details money owed -- which is what gives it a display sign.
Details
Details Business is the group that proves the side has to follow the type and
Details not the group: a receivable is owed to the business and a payable is
Details owed by it, and they sit in one group.
Examples: BalanceSideOf
| AccountType        | BalanceSide | ShownOnTheBalanceSheet |
| Checking           | Asset       | true                   |
| Savings            | Asset       | true                   |
| Cash               | Asset       | true                   |
| CreditCard         | Liability   | true                   |
| LineOfCredit       | Liability   | true                   |
| Brokerage          | Asset       | true                   |
| IRA                | Asset       | true                   |
| HSA                | Asset       | true                   |
| Retirement401k     | Asset       | true                   |
| Mortgage           | Liability   | true                   |
| AutoLoan           | Liability   | true                   |
| PersonalLoan       | Liability   | true                   |
| OtherDebt          | Liability   | true                   |
| RealEstate         | Asset       | true                   |
| Vehicle            | Asset       | true                   |
| OtherAsset         | Asset       | true                   |
| AccountsReceivable | Asset       | true                   |
| AccountsPayable    | Liability   | true                   |
| TransferIn         | Asset       | false                  |
| TransferOut        | Liability   | false                  |

Attributes BalanceSideOf
| Attribute              | Type        | Default  |
| AccountType            | AccountType | Checking |
| BalanceSide            | BalanceSide | Asset    |
| ShownOnTheBalanceSheet | Boolean     | true     |

DataType ClearedStatus
Description How far a posting has got through the records of the bank.
Details Held on the posting rather than the transaction, because a transfer
Details between two accounts clears on each statement separately.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value      | Notes                                           |
| Uncleared  | Entered here, not yet seen at the bank          |
| Cleared    | Appeared on a downloaded statement              |
| Reconciled | Locked by a finished reconciliation; edits warn |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One-attribute selectors
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description A step that names a single value still needs an attribute set, so
Description that the generated code has a struct to carry it. These are those
Description sets: one attribute each, named for what the step selects.
Details The C++ generator emits a typed and a string struct for an Attributes
Details or an Entity block and not for a bare DataType, so a step written as
Details ": AccountPath EveryCell" analyses cleanly and then generates glue that
Details will not compile. These wrappers are the fix.

Attributes AccountSelect
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Path      | AccountPath |         |

Attributes HeadingRead
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Heading   | Heading |         |

Attributes PayeeSelect
| Attribute | Type      | Default |
| Payee     | PayeeName |         |

Attributes ProfileLabel
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Name      | ProfileName |         |

Attributes SearchTerm
| Attribute | Type       | Default |
| Term      | SearchText |         |

Attributes SortBy
| Attribute | Type       | Default |
| Column    | SortColumn |         |

Attributes TransactionSelect
| Attribute | Type           | Default |
| Ref       | TransactionRef |         |
